Global Indicator Data Extractor — Multi-Source, 50-Year Daily Data
==================================================================
Extracts daily-level economic & financial indicator data going back 50 years.

Architecture mirrors the companion stock-data extractor
(Extract_ALL_Stock_Data) but targets indicators instead of equities.

Indicator Jobs (Phase 1 — 4 indicators):
  1. GOLD              — Gold spot price (USD/oz), daily since 1970s
  2. US_TREASURIES     — US Treasury yields: 1Y, 2Y, 3Y, 5Y, 7Y, 10Y, 20Y, 30Y
  3. VIX               — CBOE Volatility Index, daily since 1990
  4. OIL               — WTI Crude + Brent Crude, daily since ~1983

Data Sources (in priority order):
  • FRED (Federal Reserve Economic Data) — free API, best for yields & rates
  • Yahoo Finance (yfinance)             — commodities, VIX, bond ETFs
  • World Bank (wbgapi)                  — annual macro indicators (future phases)

Output: one CSV per indicator job in output/ directory.
  Row    = one calendar day
  Cols   = Indicator | Date columns (daily close/value)
  Format = identical to the stock extractor (transposed wide format)

Run modes:
  python extract_indicator_data.py                        # all indicators
  python extract_indicator_data.py --indicator GOLD       # single indicator
  python extract_indicator_data.py --indicator OIL
  python extract_indicator_data.py --indicator US_TREASURIES
  python extract_indicator_data.py --indicator VIX
  python extract_indicator_data.py --group commodities    # commodity group
  python extract_indicator_data.py --group rates          # rates & volatility
  python extract_indicator_data.py --list                 # show all available

FRED API Key:
  Free — register at https://fred.stlouisfed.org/docs/api/api_key.html
  Set via environment variable FRED_API_KEY or paste into the script.
  Without a FRED key, the script falls back to yfinance for everything
  (less coverage for treasury yields).

Future phases (not yet implemented):
  5. SILVER             — Silver spot price
  6. EU_BONDS           — EU sovereign yields (Germany, France, etc.)
  7. METALS             — Copper, Platinum, Palladium, Aluminum
